\pard\plain \intbl\itap1\sa180\sb180\sl300\slmult0\ql{\f3\fs20\b0\i0 Jaynes is rejecting the idea that consciousness is a passive container that merely receives experiences.}
\par\pard\plain \intbl\itap1\sa180\sb180\ql{\f3\fs20\b0\i0 Instead, he sees consciousness as an }{\f5\fs20\b1\i0 active analogical model}{\f3\fs20\b0\i0 .}
\par\plain \intbl\itap1{\f3\fs20\b0\i0 You build it from experience, but once built, it is used to organize, predict, interpret, and simulate experience.}
\par\plain \intbl\itap1{\f3\fs20\b0\i0 It's rather close to saying:}
\par\plain \intbl\itap1{\f3\fs20\b0\i0 We first construct an inner map of ourselves from our interactions with the world. Then we use that map to navigate the world, our memories, and our possible futures.}
\par\plain \intbl\itap1{\f3\fs20\b0\i0 The deepest claim in the passage is that consciousness is not primarily for perceiving the present. It is for dealing with the }{\f5\fs20\b1\i0 unknown}{\f3\fs20\b0\i0 : imagining possibilities, making decisions, reconstructing the past, and projecting oneself into situations that are not directly given. Consciousness is the map we use when the territory is not yet available.}\cell{
